	title = {Materials and applications for low-cost ceramic membranes},
	year = {2019},
	journal = {Membranes},
	volume = {9},
	number = {9},
	doi = {10.3390/membranes9090105},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85073375902&doi=10.3390%2fmembranes9090105&partnerID=40&md5=a15eb2f472c4a4328a3bcb69b7fb750c},
	abstract = {In water treatment applications, the use of ceramic membranes is associated with numerous advantages relative to polymer-based filtration systems. High-temperature stability, fouling resistance, and low maintenance requirements contribute to lower lifecycle costs in such systems. However, the high production costs of most commercially available ceramic membranes, stemming from raw materials and processing, are uneconomical for such systems in most water treatment applications. For this reason, there is a growing demand for new ceramic membranes based on low-cost raw materials and processes. The use of unrefined mineral feedstocks, clays, cement, sands, and ash as the basis for the fabrication of ceramic membranes offers a promising pathway towards the obtainment of effective filtration systems that can be economically implemented in large volumes. The design of effective ceramic filtration membranes based on low-cost raw materials and energy-efficient processes requires a balance of pore structure, mass flow, and robustness, all of which are highly dependent on the composition of materials used, the inclusion of various pore-forming and binding additives, and the thermal treatments to which membranes are subjected. In this review, we present recent developments in materials and processes for the fabrication of low-cost membranes from unrefined raw materials, including clays, zeolites, apatite, waste products, including fly ash and rice husk ash, and cement. We examine multiple aspects of materials design and address the challenges relating to their further development. © 2019 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.},
	author_keywords = {Fly ash; Inorganic membranes; Kaolin; Low-cost ceramic membrane; Oil-water separation; Rice husk ash; Water filtration},
	keywords = {Additives; Cements; Ceramic materials; Ceramic membranes; Clay; Costs; Energy efficiency; Fly ash; Kaolin; Life cycle; Microfiltration; Phosphate minerals; Pore structure; Water filtration; Water treatment; Zeolites; Energy-efficient process; High temperature stability; Inorganic membranes; Low cost ceramic membrane; Low-cost raw materials; Materials and process; Oil water separation; Rice husk ash; Membranes},
