	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-33746784819&doi=10.1073%2fpnas.0604964103&partnerID=40&md5=ffbad1ef40d723436bbff880bb6c334a},
	abstract = {Reinforced concrete structures exposed to aggressive environments often require repair or retrofit even though they were designed to last >50 years. This statement is especially true for structures subjected to sulfate attack. It is critical that fundamental models of life prediction be developed for durability of concrete. Based on experimental results obtained over a 40-year period, scaling and saturation laws were formulated for concrete exposed to sulfate solution. These features have not been considered in current models used to predict life cycle of concrete exposed to aggressive environment. The mathematical analysis shows that porous concrete made with high and moderate water-to-cement ratios develops a definite scaling law after an initiation time. The scaling coefficient depends on the cement composition but does not depend on the original water-to-cement ratio. Dense concrete made with low water-to-cement ratios develops a cyclic saturation curve. An index for "potential of damage" is created to allow engineers to design concrete structures with better precision and cement chemists to develop portland cements with optimized composition. © 2006 by The National Academy of Sciences of the USA.},
	author_keywords = {Asymptotics; Damage; Diffusion; Model; Reaction},
